 "segments": [
  {
   "start": 0.2,
   "end": 1.92,
   "text": "Let's test the obvious answer first."
  },
  {
   "start": 2.22,
   "end": 5.74,
   "text": "Theory one: the wall was a giant fortress, built to stop an invasion."
  },
  {
   "start": 6.12,
   "end": 6.62,
   "text": "Problem."
  },
  {
   "start": 6.88,
   "end": 12.26,
   "text": "A stone wall maybe fifteen feet high is impressive, but raiders with a ladder can get over it in a minute."
  },
  {
   "start": 12.48,
   "end": 20.86,
   "text": "Seventy-three miles is far too long to guard every yard at once. Historians aren't even sure there was a walkway along the top for soldiers to fight from."
  },
  {
   "start": 21.5,
   "end": 23.92,
   "text": "And Rome didn't like fighting from behind walls anyway."
  },
  {
   "start": 24.28,
   "end": 27.2,
   "text": "Its whole style was to march out and win in the open,"
  },
  {
   "start": 27.38,
   "end": 28.8,
   "text": "where training and armor mattered."
  },
  {
   "start": 28.98,
   "end": 33.82,
   "text": "Hiding behind stone was the opposite of how Rome fought. So the wall did something smarter."
  },
  {
   "start": 34.12,
   "end": 35.18,
   "text": "It didn't stop raiders."
  },
  {
   "start": 35.58,
   "end": 36.56,
   "text": "It slowed them down."
  },
  {
   "start": 36.86,
   "end": 37.54,
   "text": "Picture a raid."
  },
  {
   "start": 37.88,
   "end": 39.64,
   "text": "A band of warriors crosses the ditch,"
  },
  {
   "start": 39.88,
   "end": 40.66,
   "text": "climbs the wall,"
  },
  {
   "start": 40.84,
   "end": 41.72,
   "text": "and grabs some cattle."
  },
  {
   "start": 42.12,
   "end": 46.68,
   "text": "Now they have to get home, with cows, over a wall and a ditch. Cows are terrible at ladders."
  },
  {
   "start": 46.9,
   "end": 47.44,
   "text": "Meanwhile,"
  },
  {
   "start": 47.66,
   "end": 49.22,
   "text": "a guard in a turret has spotted them."
  },
  {
   "start": 49.74,
   "end": 52.58,
   "text": "Signals probably pass along the line from tower to tower."
  },
  {
   "start": 52.94,
   "end": 59.58,
   "text": "Cavalry rides out from the nearest fort, through a gate, and catches the raiders in the open, exactly where Rome wanted to fight."
  },
  {
   "start": 59.76,
   "end": 61.38,
   "text": "The wall was a tripwire and a trap."
  },
  {
   "start": 61.64,
   "end": 64.56,
   "text": "It turned a quick raid into a slow, noisy mistake."
  },
  {
   "start": 64.86,
   "end": 66.56,
   "text": "Rome didn't need to stop every raid."
  },
  {
   "start": 66.8,
   "end": 69.02,
   "text": "It just needed to make every raid a bad idea."
  },
  {
   "start": 69.26,
   "end": 70.72,
   "text": "But that's still only half the story."
  },
  {
   "start": 70.98,
   "end": 78.0,
   "text": "Because most people who came to the wall weren't raiders at all. So here's the real answer. Hadrian's Wall was less like a fortress,"
  },
  {
   "start": 78.2,
   "end": 79.96,
   "text": "and more like a border checkpoint."
  },
  {
   "start": 80.36,
   "end": 81.88,
   "text": "Think less Great Wall of China,"
  },
  {
   "start": 82.12,
   "end": 83.14,
   "text": "more airport security."
  },
  {
   "start": 83.48,
   "end": 86.88,
   "text": "Remember the man with the goat? He walks up to a milecastle gate."
  },
  {
   "start": 87.16,
   "end": 89.2,
   "text": "A soldier looks him over. Where are you going?"
  },
  {
   "start": 89.54,
   "end": 90.36,
   "text": "What are you carrying?"
  },
  {
   "start": 90.58,
   "end": 91.28,
   "text": "Are you armed?\""
  },
  {
   "start": 91.5,
   "end": 96.06,
   "text": "Many historians think travelers and traders paid a fee to cross, like customs today."
  },
  {
   "start": 96.32,
   "end": 97.62,
   "text": "Nobody slips through unseen."
  },
  {
   "start": 97.86,
   "end": 99.32,
   "text": "Every person, every cart,"
  },
  {
   "start": 99.62,
   "end": 102.0,
   "text": "every goat goes through a door Rome controls."
  },
  {
   "start": 102.2,
   "end": 104.44,
   "text": "And remember the Vallum, the ditch that faced south?"
  },
  {
   "start": 105.12,
   "end": 109.46,
   "text": "It meant even people inside the province couldn't walk up to the wall wherever they liked."
  },
  {
   "start": 109.66,
   "end": 111.72,
   "text": "The wall wasn't built to keep everyone out."
  },
  {
   "start": 112.02,
   "end": 113.56,
   "text": "It was built to decide who got in."
  },
  {
   "start": 113.86,
   "end": 115.62,
   "text": "And there's one more job the wall did."
  },
  {
   "start": 115.9,
   "end": 116.7,
   "text": "It sent a message."
  },
  {
   "start": 116.88,
   "end": 121.14,
   "text": "Hadrian's own biography says he built it to separate the Romans from the barbarians."
  },
  {
   "start": 121.36,
   "end": 123.6,
   "text": "A line on the ground that said: this side is Rome."
  },
  {
   "start": 123.92,
   "end": 124.86,
   "text": "Roads, taxes,"
  },
  {
   "start": 125.1,
   "end": 125.24,
   "text": "baths,"
  },
  {
   "start": 125.58,
   "end": 125.86,
   "text": "law."
  },
  {
   "start": 126.12,
   "end": 128.72,
   "text": "That side is not. It also sent a message back home."
  },
  {
   "start": 128.96,
   "end": 131.64,
   "text": "After centuries of conquest, the empire would stop here."
  },
  {
   "start": 131.86,
   "end": 133.72,
   "text": "No more marching into the Highlands."
  },
  {
   "start": 133.9,
   "end": 135.76,
   "text": "No more endless wars over cold hills."
  },
  {
   "start": 136.02,
   "end": 139.68,
   "text": "The wall was Hadrian putting a period at the end of a very long sentence."
  },
  {
   "start": 139.86,
   "end": 142.92,
   "text": "And some historians add a very practical bonus."
  },
  {
   "start": 143.1,
   "end": 145.88,
   "text": "Thousands of bored soldiers on a quiet frontier are trouble."
  },
  {
   "start": 146.16,
   "end": 148.32,
   "text": "Thousands of busy soldiers are builders."
  },
  {
   "start": 148.58,
   "end": 149.22,
   "text": "Did it work?"
  },
  {
   "start": 149.5,
   "end": 149.92,
   "text": "Mostly."
  },
  {
   "start": 150.34,
   "end": 152.14,
   "text": "Hadrian died in 138, and his successor,"
  },
  {
   "start": 152.48,
   "end": 154.12,
   "text": "Antoninus Pius,"
  },
  {
   "start": 154.3,
   "end": 155.64,
   "text": "tried pushing north again."
  },
  {
   "start": 155.92,
   "end": 159.34,
   "text": "Around 142 he began a new wall of turf across Scotland,"
  },
  {
   "start": 159.8,
   "end": 161.42,
   "text": "about thirty-nine miles long."
  },
  {
   "start": 161.68,
   "end": 163.76,
   "text": "It lasted only about twenty years."
  },
  {
   "start": 163.96,
   "end": 166.82,
   "text": "Then Rome pulled back south to Hadrian's line, and stayed there."
  },
  {
   "start": 167.14,
   "end": 174.88,
   "text": "Hadrian's Wall was used, repaired, and rebuilt for nearly three centuries, until Roman rule in Britain ended around the year 410."
  },
  {
   "start": 175.2,
   "end": 175.74,
   "text": "After that,"
  },
  {
   "start": 176.0,
   "end": 178.04,
   "text": "local farmers did what farmers do."
  },
  {
   "start": 178.4,
   "end": 179.44,
   "text": "They took the free stone."
  },
  {
   "start": 179.74,
   "end": 184.66,
   "text": "You can still find Roman blocks in churches, barns, and field walls across the north of England."
  },
  {
   "start": 184.96,
   "end": 188.72,
   "text": "In the 1750s, the British army even built a road right on top of it,"
  },
  {
   "start": 189.02,
   "end": 190.9,
   "text": "using the wall as a foundation."
  },
  {
   "start": 191.12,
   "end": 196.96,
   "text": "Today, Hadrian's Wall is a World Heritage Site. You can walk its whole length on an eighty-four-mile trail."
  },
  {
   "start": 197.3,
   "end": 200.52,
   "text": "Here's one for trivia night: it isn't the border between England and Scotland."
  },
  {
   "start": 201.04,
   "end": 202.88,
   "text": "The whole wall sits inside England."
  },
  {
   "start": 203.16,
   "end": 205.98,
   "text": "In 1981, an American writer visited it, stood on top,"
  },
  {
   "start": 206.16,
   "end": 208.66,
   "text": "and imagined a guard staring north into nothing."
  },
  {
   "start": 208.9,
   "end": 209.34,
   "text": "Years later,"
  },
  {
   "start": 209.84,
   "end": 214.3,
   "text": "he turned that feeling into a giant wall of ice in a fantasy series. So,"
  },
  {
   "start": 214.58,
   "end": 216.66,
   "text": "why did Rome build a wall across Britain?"
  },
  {
   "start": 216.96,
   "end": 219.26,
   "text": "Not because the mightiest empire on Earth was scared."
  },
  {
   "start": 219.5,
   "end": 222.76,
   "text": "It wanted something better than a fortress. It wanted a door."
  },
  {
   "start": 223.04,
   "end": 223.68,
   "text": "And a doorman."
  },
  {
   "start": 223.94,
   "end": 224.38,
   "text": "And a toll."
  },
  {
   "start": 224.58,
   "end": 226.22,
   "text": "The goat, by the way, still hasn't paid."
  }
 ],
